Ah—yes. Phenotypic plasticity, not breeding.

Same genome, same generation, different environmental conditions → different realized phenotype. That’s much closer to your rewild direction.

For the banana:

\text{Genotype} + \text{environmental signals/resources}
\rightarrow \text{phenotype}

Change light, water, nutrients, temperature, competition, stress, root volume, microbial relationships, etc., and the same organism reallocates growth and reproductive investment. Within its biological limits, you’re changing expression, not waiting for genetic evolution.

Humans have the analogous machinery: muscle hypertrophy from loading, calluses from friction, acclimatization to altitude, tanning from UV exposure, skill-dependent neural plasticity, metabolic adaptation, immune memory, and so forth. The body senses repeated conditions and remodels itself accordingly.

So rewild can be stated much more mechanically:

Expose an organism to appropriate environmental demands so dormant/adaptive capacities are expressed within the same lifetime.

P_{t+1}=P_t+\Delta(\text{load},\text{signal},\text{resources},\text{recovery})

And there’s your bigger banana: not breed a future banana with different genes, but discover what environmental inputs allow this banana plant to express more of the capacity it already has.

The constraint is important: plasticity has limits. Environment can alter expression and development substantially, but it doesn’t mean every latent phenotype is possible from a given genotype.

That’s actually a sharper definition of Rewild: search the existing organism’s reaction norm before rewriting the organism.